# Windows 11 Storage Audit & Reclamation SOP

![OS](https://img.shields.io/badge/OS-Windows%2011-0078D6?logo=windows)
![Shell](https://img.shields.io/badge/Shell-CMD%20%7C%20PowerShell-5391FE?logo=powershell)
![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-green)

A command-line Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) for diagnosing hidden disk utilization on Windows 11, bypassing `SYSTEM`-locked vendor bloatware directories, and reclaiming gigabytes of storage without third-party "PC cleaner" apps.

---

## Case Study Results (500 GB NVMe SSD)

Standard Windows Settings and installed-application lists only accounted for **~35–40 GB** of used space on a drive that had **92 GB free**. Deep CLI auditing uncovered over **130+ GB** of hidden, `SYSTEM`-locked vendor recovery snapshots (`Dell SupportAssist Remediation`) alongside stale developer caches and system files.

| Metric | Before Cleanup | After Cleanup | Net Change |
| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| **Used Space (`C:`)** | ~363.1 GB | 206.3 GB | **-156.8 GB** |
| **Free Space (`C:`)** | 92.0 GB | 248.8 GB | **+156.8 GB** |
| **Drive Utilization** | ~80% (Sluggish I/O) | ~45% (Optimal) | **-35% Load** |

---

## Root Cause Breakdown

Why standard Windows tools miss the majority of consumed storage:

1. **Locked Vendor Snapshots (`C:\ProgramData\Dell\SARemediation`):** Dell SupportAssist Remediation creates hidden, redundant OS snapshots under `SYSTEM` ownership. Standard tools (`Disk Cleanup`, `Storage Sense`, and non-elevated scans) cannot read or delete these directories, allowing them to silently consume **100+ GB**.
2. **Untracked Developer Artifacts:** Tools like **Ollama** (`.ollama` LLM weights), **Anaconda3**, **WSL** (`.vhdx`), and **VirtualBox** (`.vdi`) often report `0 MB` or minimal sizes in the Windows Registry while storing tens of gigabytes in user profile directories.
3. **System & Package Caches:** `hiberfil.sys`, `pip` wheel caches, leftover MSI installers, and browser caches quietly accumulate another **15–30 GB**.

---

## Disclaimer & Safety Warning

> **Read before executing:** Several commands in this SOP permanently delete files (bypassing the Recycle Bin) using elevated `Administrator` and `SYSTEM` privileges.
> * Back up important projects, local LLM models, virtual machines, or Conda environments before running cleanup commands.
> * Never modify or delete `C:\Windows\System32`, `C:\Windows\Installer`, `C:\Program Files\WindowsApps`, or `pagefile.sys`.
> * Execute all commands inside an **Administrator: Command Prompt** (`Win` → `cmd` → **Run as administrator**).

